    {
      "id": "e09",
      "time_offset_ms": 159516,
      "type": "show_result",
      "target": "sql_viewer",
      "params": {},
      "narration": "Two churn events in July. A rate of zero point zero one. One percent. Not five percent. One percent. The board was told churn was five percent in July. The churn events table says one percent. That is a difference of five times. Think about what decisions get made around a number like that. Do we need to intervene on customer retention? Is the product team under pressure? Is the sales team being asked to compensate for churn that is actually a fraction of what was reported? All of that downstream consequence starts here, with two tables that answer the same question differently, and no documentation to explain why.",
      "transition": "emphasis",
      "clears": [],
      "phases": []
    },
